(2403505, 322030, 39088, '<td valign="top">Greetings TO the Critique Club.  Thank you for letting me know the proper decorum on commenting on my own work.  I want you to know I find this very difficult to do.  I am a very new photographer and am apprehensive to look like I either, don''t know what the heck I am talking about or, even more dreadfull, look like I think I know it all when in fact I know nothing.  That bieng said I am not very happy with this photo the DOF seems far to shallow and leaves too much of the flower just slightly out of focus.  I do however think that my croping is quite creative and I enjoy how the black complements the yellow and assists in making the subject stand out.  I have yet to fiqure out how to get that incredibly crisp focus that I have seen others attain so perfectly.  Perhaps you can give me some clues.  I have always owned point and shoot and just recently have purchased a D70, the controll is daunting and I find something new every time I take it out.<br/> <br/>Thank you<br/>Troutbear</td>', 'Greetings TO the Critique Club.  Thank you for letting me know the proper decorum on commenting on my own work.  I want you to know I find this very difficult to do.  I am a very new photographer and am apprehensive to look like I either, don''t know what the heck I am talking about or, even more dreadfull, look like I think I know it all when in fact I know nothing.  That bieng said I am not very happy with this photo the DOF seems far to shallow and leaves too much of the flower just slightly out of focus.  I do however think that my croping is quite creative and I enjoy how the black complements the yellow and assists in making the subject stand out.  I have yet to fiqure out how to get that incredibly crisp focus that I have seen others attain so perfectly.  Perhaps you can give me some clues.  I have always owned point and shoot and just recently have purchased a D70, the controll is daunting and I find something new every time I take it out. Thank youTroutbear', '2006-05-10 20:40:52.000000', NULL, 0),
